course: dictionary
lesson: 13106
title: Flapping: wings, washing and worry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one you need. Look at the words around «flapping». «The seagull was flapping its wings over the harbour.» The wings are moving up and down quickly, so flapping means moving wings up and down.
explain: Now read this: «The washing was flapping on the line all afternoon.» Nothing here is moving up and down. The washing moves loosely back and forth in the wind, like a flag. So flapping can mean this too.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence. Some words also carry a feeling. «Stop flapping, the bus is only five minutes away.» Flapping here is informal British English, and it means being nervous and worried, in a flap.
fact: Flapping means moving wings or arms up and down quickly.
fact: Flapping can also mean moving loosely back and forth, like a flag or a sail in the wind.
fact: In informal British English, flapping can mean being nervous and worried. Someone who is worried is in a flap.
fact: Birds fly by flapping their wings, but some birds glide without flapping.
fact: The phrases «flapping wings», «flapping in the wind» and «stop flapping» each show a different meaning of the word.
exercise: «The eagle was flapping its huge wings to take off.» What is the eagle doing? | Lying still on the ground | *Moving its wings up and down quickly | Sleeping on a tree | Singing a loud song
exercise: «The washing was flapping on the line in the wind.» What does flapping mean here? | Pulling the line down with both hands | Shouting for help | *Moving loosely back and forth in the wind | Being very hungry
exercise: «Stop flapping, the bus is only five minutes away.» What does flapping mean here? | Flying like a bird | *Being nervous and worried | Sleeping peacefully | Running around the bus stop
test: «The flag was flapping on the tall pole.» What is the flag doing? | Being folded away for the night | *Moving loosely back and forth in the wind | Lying flat and still | Changing colour in the sun
test: «Dad was flapping about the lost keys, and in the end they were in his pocket.» What does flapping mean here? | Waving at a friend across the road | Swimming in the sea | *Being nervous and worried, in a flap | Moving his arms up and down quickly
exam: «The sail was flapping as the wind dropped, and the boat drifted slowly.» Which meaning fits best? | Moving wings up and down quickly | Being nervous and worried | Standing perfectly still | *Moving loosely back and forth, with the wind now weak
